{"schema":1,"slug":"potawatomi-great-lakes-and-the-ohio-valley-wars","nation":"Potawatomi","baseRef":"/data/web/territories/_base-great-lakes.json","yearStart":1640,"yearEnd":1809,"presenceLabel":"Potawatomi town or homeland area (general)","presenceNote":"The Potawatomi homeland is the Great Lakes (Michigan, Wisconsin, northern Indiana and Illinois), outside Kentucky and mostly outside this map, so it is described in text and not drawn. The story itself has no dates; the page spans c.1640 (Beaver Wars refuge) to 1809 (Fort Wayne cession). The Kentucky link is the 1791 Scott and Wilkinson expeditions against Wea and Miami neighbours, and Potawatomi warriors in the Confederacy; the story names no Potawatomi raid on a Kentucky station.","confidence":"low","approximate":true,"overlapNote":"Miami, Wea, Kickapoo, Ojibwe and Odawa (Three Fires), Illinois (their country was taken from the Illinois), Menominee and Ho-Chunk in Wisconsin. In the 1805 Grouseland treaty they acknowledged the Delaware right to sell; in 1809 they ceded with the Delaware, Miami and Eel River.","basis":"Illinois State Museum (Midewin exhibit), NHBP timeline, Indiana DNR, Forest County Potawatomi (Wisconsin DPI), Greenville and Fort Wayne treaty references.","basisChip":"ethnohistory","timeline":{"min":1640,"max":1810},"fadeYears":0,"fadeSouth":false,"view":{"full":"40 47 540 418","narrow":"40 47 540 418","card":"40 47 540 418"},"mapLabelPos":[-86.5,43.3],"pins":[],"startEra":{"potawatomi-fire-keepers-and-the-ohio-valley-wars":"homeland"},"stories":{"potawatomi-fire-keepers-and-the-ohio-valley-wars":{"yearStart":1640,"yearEnd":1809,"presenceLabel":"Potawatomi town or homeland area (general)","approxStart":true}},"legend":[{"kind":"soft","label":"Approximate general area of Potawatomi presence (whole counties, not a boundary)"},{"kind":"point","label":"Site (exact position)"},{"kind":"ring","label":"Site, location approximate"}],"eras":[{"id":"beaver","label":"c.1640–1670 · Beaver Wars refuge","short":"c.1640","yearStart":1640,"yearEnd":1670,"confidence":"low","sources":["ism_pot"],"storySlugs":["potawatomi-fire-keepers-and-the-ohio-valley-wars"],"text":"By the 1640s the Potawatomi were under Iroquois attack and fled north to the Straits of Mackinac and to Green Bay and Door County, Wisconsin, where they built French trade alliances and, by 1670, began to expand (Illinois State Museum). The tint is an approximate general area (Green Bay and Door County, whole counties), not a boundary.","layers":[{"feature":"pot_gb","style":"soft"}],"apprTag":"Approximate general area","mapLabel":"POTAWATOMI","mapSubLabel":"approximate general area · c.1640–1670","mapLabelPos":[-88.0,45.7]},{"id":"homeland","label":"c.1763–1795 · Southern Michigan, northern Indiana and Illinois","short":"c.1763","yearStart":1763,"yearEnd":1794,"confidence":"low","sources":["nhbp","dnr_in","ism_pot","greenville"],"storySlugs":["potawatomi-fire-keepers-and-the-ohio-valley-wars"],"text":"The Detroit Potawatomi left their river villages in 1763 and set up villages on the Huron River and the Raisin; Indiana DNR has Potawatomi moving as far south as the Wabash in the 1770s. By the 1800s there were over 100 villages, 14 of them in northern and central Illinois (Illinois State Museum). The tint is an approximate general area of southwest and southeast Michigan, northern Indiana and northeast Illinois, built from whole counties: no source gives a boundary. Potawatomi warriors stood in the Northwest Indian Confederacy; Kentucky’s 1791 columns under Scott and Wilkinson struck their Wea and Miami neighbours, not Potawatomi towns.","layers":[{"feature":"pot_home","style":"soft"},{"feature":"detroit","style":"point"},{"feature":"chicago","style":"point"}],"apprTag":"Approximate general area · Detroit and Chicago points approximate","mapLabel":"POTAWATOMI","mapSubLabel":"approximate general area · c.1763–1794","mapLabelPos":[-86.5,43.3]},{"id":"greenville","label":"1795–1809 · Greenville and the Chicago cession","short":"1795","yearStart":1795,"yearEnd":1809,"confidence":"medium","sources":["greenville","ebsco_fw","fcp"],"storySlugs":[],"text":"At Greenville (3 Aug 1795) the Potawatomi signed, took $1,000 a year and ceded six miles square at Chicago among the posts. On 30 Sept 1809 at Fort Wayne the Delaware, Potawatomi, Miami and Eel River ceded about 2.5 million acres along the Wabash (EBSCO; acreage varies by source, 2.5 to 3 million). The tint is an approximate general area, widened to northern and central Illinois and the upper Wabash where villages lay by the 1800s; it is not a boundary.","layers":[{"feature":"pot_later","style":"soft"},{"feature":"chicago","style":"point"}],"notDrawn":{"map":"POTAWATOMI HOMELAND: GREAT LAKES · NOT DRAWN","tag":"Homeland outside the map window; text only"},"apprTag":"Approximate general area · Chicago point approximate","mapLabel":"POTAWATOMI","mapSubLabel":"approximate general area · 1795–1809","mapLabelPos":[-87.3,43.3]}],"sources":[{"id":"ism_pot","label":"The Potawatomi (Illinois State Museum, Midewin exhibit)","url":"https://museum.state.il.us/exhibits/midewin/potowatomi.html"},{"id":"nhbp","label":"1763: Detroit Potawatomi leave Detroit (Nottawaseppi Huron Band of the Potawatomi)","url":"https://nhbp-nsn.gov/timeline/1763/"},{"id":"dnr_in","label":"Historical Indians of Indiana (Indiana DNR, PDF)","url":"https://www.in.gov/dnr/kids/files/historical-indians.pdf"},{"id":"fcp","label":"Forest County Potawatomi (Wisconsin DPI)","url":"https://dpi.state.wi.us/amind/tribalnationswi/fcp"},{"id":"greenville","label":"Treaty of Greenville, 3 Aug 1795 (Avalon Project)","url":"https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/greenvil.asp"},{"id":"ebsco_fw","label":"Treaty of Fort Wayne 1809 (EBSCO Research Starters)","url":"https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/treaty-fort-wayne/"}],"mainMap":{"general":"mm_general","points":"mm_points","labels":[{"text":"Potawatomi: approximate general area c.1640–1809 (Green Bay, then Michigan, northern Indiana and Illinois). Low confidence","at":[-87.2,43.4]}]},"geojson":{"type":"FeatureCollection","features":[{"type":"Feature","properties":{"id":"detroit","note":"Detroit (Wikipedia 42.3317, -83.0459). NHBP: the Detroit Potawatomi left their river villages in Oct 1763 and spread south and west; a 1771 deed at their ancient village is recorded.","approximate":true,"label":"Detroit","dx":9,"dy":5,"anchor":"start","name":"Detroit"},"geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-83.0459,42.3317]}},{"type":"Feature","properties":{"id":"chicago","note":"Chicago (Wikipedia 41.8819, -87.6278). Greenville Art. 3: six miles square at the mouth of the Chikago river where a fort formerly stood, and twelve miles square near the Illinois mouth, were ceded. Potawatomi ceded lands around Chicago later (the 1833 treaty is not covered here).","approximate":true,"label":"Chicago","dx":9,"dy":5,"anchor":"start","name":"Chicago"},"geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-87.6278,41.8819]}},{"type":"Feature","properties":{"id":"extent","kind":"main-map extent","approximate":true,"note":"Main-map extent: general areas (whole-county shapes, approximate), solid lines in Kentucky and sourced points."},"geometry":{"type":"GeometryCollection","geometries":[{"type":"MultiPolygon","coordinates":[[[[-89.8623,41.584],[-89.632,41.587],[-89.629,41.9264],[-89.6854,41.9303],[-89.6885,42.1991],[-89.3974,42.2031],[-89.4014,42.5004],[-87.8005,42.4919],[-87.8054,42.3847],[-87.8347,42.3022],[-87.8285,42.2699],[-87.7417,42.1283],[-87.6715,42.0583],[-87.6117,41.8922],[-87.6094,41.8452],[-87.5761,41.7857],[-87.5307,41.7482],[-87.5105,41.6961],[-87.4385,41.6707],[-87.4293,41.6458],[-87.2778,41.6198],[-87.1183,41.6463],[-86.9353,41.7095],[-86.6792,41.8449],[-86.5981,41.9181],[-86.4852,42.1182],[-86.3564,42.254],[-86.2739,42.4193],[-84.7185,42.4215],[-84.7078,42.071],[-84.136,42.0716],[-84.1311,42.4246],[-82.8704,42.4509],[-82.924,42.3521],[-83.0639,42.3178],[-83.0965,42.2901],[-83.1281,42.2383],[-83.1335,4